A||N||34||Will||Survey: single hardest bolt||||1||
Z||000000||Will||03-22-2001||10:59 PM||||What is the one most difficult, most PITA bolt, nut, or other fastener to deal with on a Fiero, and under what conditions (for example some bolts are easy with cradle out, but terrible with it in), and what tool setup do you use for it (15mm deep socket w/U-joint and 6 inch extension for instance)?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||DJRice||03-22-2001||11:25 PM||||Both Upper A/C Compressor Bolts on a V-6 with the engine in.<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<P>"Who wants somma Debo?"||||reg||1
Z||000002||AkursedX||03-22-2001||11:45 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DJRice:<BR><B>Both Upper A/C Compressor Bolts on a V-6 with the engine in.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>DARN!!!!!! You beat me to it!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I needed a 3ft extension, with a swivel head socket to get at those damn things!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P><BR>Edit: poor grammar<BR><p>[This message has been edited by AkursedX (edited 03-22-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000003||voyagerspe||03-22-2001||11:52 PM||||ditto ditto ditto<BR>I took the whole bracket off.  I fought with this bolt for 5 hours before I thought about removing the bracket.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||03-23-2001||07:46 AM||||I'd have to agree with the A/C bolt.||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||03-23-2001||08:06 AM||||the exhaust manifold bolt behind the A/C bracket on my V6. or the angled oil pan bolts at the front of the motor...  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||03-23-2001||08:44 AM||||I never got along well with the pivot bolt on Fiero alternators.  On my V6, I needed 3 ft of extensions so the ratchet could swing in the empty space on the driver's side, but once I set it up that way, it came out fine.  <P>On my 84 4-banger, I could not break that bolt free no matter what I tried and had to send the car to a mechanic, who used a torch.<P>The original alternator in my 88 Coupe is still working fine (knock on wood).||||reg||8
Z||000007||Raydar||03-23-2001||09:37 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>I never got along well with the pivot bolt on Fiero alternators.  On my V6, I needed 3 ft of extensions so the ratchet could swing in the empty space on the driver's side, but once I set it up that way, it came out fine.  <P>On my 84 4-banger, I could not break that bolt free no matter what I tried and had to send the car to a mechanic, who used a torch.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Same story with my top alternator bolt.<BR>Once I was sure the bolt wasn't frozen in place, I used an impact wrench.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>(When skill and finesse don't work, there's always ignorance and brute force.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jay||03-23-2001||10:04 AM||||Well this is not a bolt, nut or screw but a cap.  Try doing an oil change on the northstar engine in a fiero.  The cap for the oil filler is pushed up against a hinge and the fire wall.  Once you get the cap off, good luck trying to get a funnel in there.  I want to make a funnel but it's going to look like a 200 foot water slide that does loops!!  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||LZeitgeist||03-23-2001||10:06 AM||||Oh, the price you pay for horsepower...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tigger||03-23-2001||10:55 AM||||To pick another bolt that's tricky, I'd say the sailpanel nut (for the notchbacks).  The one you have to get to from the inside is a pain.  The access hole isn't much bigger than the socket needed to get it out.  It's real easy to drop the socket or the nut down in the subframe.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Will||03-23-2001||11:13 AM||||Has anyone else noticed that Fieros eat tools? I have never dropped a socket or wrench into a Fiero engine compartment and had it fall out the bottom like it should. I can usually find the wrenches, but I never see the sockets again. I'm down by three or four sockets from working on Fieros. When I drop a tool into my car, I don't hear clink-clink-clink-clak; instead I hear clink-clink-slurp-gulp and never see the tool again.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Voytek||03-23-2001||11:42 AM||||I found a socket and 2 screws I lost when I took the top of my engine apart to do some injector cleaning.  Never found an exhaust bolt that was to be used on a new Sprint manifold, though (I'm sure one day it'll come up (or down?)).  The AC compressor bolts have to be the worst, though - I had to CUT a 15mm wrench to adjust the belt on my AC compressor, only to find that the AC does not work!||||reg||1
Z||000013||AkursedX||03-23-2001||03:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>Has anyone else noticed that Fieros eat tools? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I once dropped a socket near the battery, and it fell into some sort of a crack that must lead to another dimension.  I have looked everywhere in the engine compartment for that socket!!  The funny thing is, when I go into a turn kinda hard, I can hear it rolling around somewhere! lol  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||KRMFiero||03-23-2001||04:38 PM||||torque converter bolts are hard to take off ||||reg||1
Z||000015||RRobinson||03-23-2001||06:36 PM||||About fieros eating tools...<BR>I could stock an autoparts store with all the stuff that I've dropped into the engine compartment. It NEVER just falls out nicely onto the floor. It's always an Indiana Jones style adventure searching for the lost socket, nut, bolt etc.<BR>I feel your pain.<BR>Ryan ||||reg||1
Z||000016||rogergarrison||03-23-2001||08:00 PM||||Id HAVE to say ALL the exhaust manifold bolts. just when you think its gonna bust loose it breaks.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Jay||03-24-2001||10:25 AM||||I like that "Indiana Jones style adventure" that about sums it up!||||reg||1
Z||000018||Fiero_Freak||03-24-2001||11:47 AM||||Ahhh  This has just made my morning....I'm laughing my ass off starting with Will's post.  I haven't had the pleasure of most of these bolts but I have done the front of the oil pan.  This is why you should have Carnies.  Like Austin Powers says, "...small hands."||||reg||1
Z||000019||StuGood||03-24-2001||05:21 PM||||Hardest bolt to remove on Fieros?  Without question - the power steering pump attach bolts  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  I've <B>never</B> been able to get <B>any</B> of them off  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||12
Z||000020||prof bobo||03-26-2001||02:36 PM||||Rear cradle bolts.  Rusted.  Frozen.  Busted socket.  Busted replacement socket from Sears.  Heated red hot.  Impact hammer rounded off heads.  Neighbors thought I was maybe making my own horseshoes and invited Joe Pesce over for (loud) commentary.  Those 2" FLEXIBLE carbide cutoff wheels from Dremel are worth twice the price.  I'm still trying to figure out what to do when it comes time to put it all back together.  (Two headless bolts sticking out the bottom of the frame and I think the nuts on the frame have broken their welds -if they were)||||reg||1
Z||000021||filthyscarecrow||03-26-2001||06:25 PM||||i had an 85gt that i changed the struts on. the 2 bolts that hold the strut to the upright were a dirty biotch to get out. my dad let me use his tools which included a hardened adapter for the breaker bar. it was made out of vanadium steel/cromoly/tungsten/adamantium(or whatever woverine's skeleton is made out of)- the point being that it was supposed to be unbreakable. i broke it. twisted it off like a bottlecap. after i was done swearing and kicking the car i called my dad and he came over to help. i believe his exact words were "hoooooooooooly sh!t!!!" i was afraid he'd be mad. he was more amazed at it than anything. so i just used a pipe wrence on the socket and a couple liters of propane to heat it up. as soon as it came out we had a little victory celebration. needless to say i bathed the bolts in anti-seize before it went back together...<P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000022||tgowens||03-27-2001||12:25 AM||||StuGood, you are TOO Good!! -- you are right, those bolts ARE hard to get off but, not nearly as hard as the upper rear horn strut...||||reg||1
Z||000023||Homer||03-27-2001||03:19 AM||||Sorry ya'll got it wrong....try the door panel retaining screw that is located in the door jam uptop near the hinge....it's coated with that plastic crap and requires a six point tip that will always strip the b@#ch out.... Don't beleive?  Go out and see if yours breaks loose of strips.  Once it strips theres basically nothing you can do to get that thing out short of getting a blow torch and melting all of the plastic off to get at the aluminum screw underneath that has been striped out too. Then just try and get a pair of vicegrips in there to get ahold of the sucker. When you do get a lock on it NOW WHAT? There isn't any room to unscrew the b@s@t@rd .  That was my delema anyway and the toughest bolt and or screw to deal with.<P>Just my 2 cents.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/CHAINSAW.GIF">  <P><p>[This message has been edited by Homer (edited 03-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||80's BOY||03-27-2001||12:24 PM||||sorry, i think you all have it easy so far. you guys with v6 engines, look at your temp sensor on the head clostest to the trunk. well, an autometer manual gage doesn't have enough tubing to reach that. sooooooo....my toughest bolt story is look on the opposite side of the other head closest to the deck lid hinges on the right and there is a little cap where you can install another temp gage sensor. THAT SOMBITCH IS HARD TO GET OUT!!! after i removed it, i actually BEAT IT ON AN ANVIL until it was a flat piece of pulp. then i threw it off a bridge. try it.. it really does make you feel better. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000025||btoth||03-27-2001||01:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>Has anyone else noticed that Fieros eat tools?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I lost 3 15mm sockets (one was a deep socket, too), two distributor hold down clamps, and a 10mm socket.  They all fell down the same way - by the transmission, towards the trunk.  Never saw any of them again.  All I would hear was a 'dink' and then nothing.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000026||GT GT 87||03-27-2001||05:58 PM||||I am freaked to admit this,,,, This is but this is my third GT ,that I have changed rear struts on .And I have had cuts getting to MANY of the tough Fiero fasteners.  (try both door lock cyl. with a large hand). But it took 8 Hrs. to get those large ,easily accesabe , camber ,strut bolts out. At 110000 miles they were original, can you imagine how much better it rides? I think I got screwed on the wheel alin.  though!!!!!!!!!!          STEVE||||reg||1
Z||000027||Tigger||03-29-2001||10:08 AM||||Lower control arm bolts, period!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000028||Eric_PA||03-29-2001||10:36 AM||||Definately the lower control arm bolts. <P>The front aren't that bad unless you have to replace them the same day. Such as in my case this week. The rear on the other hand, what a pain in the but. I forgot to mention that I recently broke all four bolts that hold my sway bar on. I have to drill them all out of the frame. Wish me luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Eric||||reg||1
Z||000029||StuGood||03-29-2001||11:25 AM||||On a serious note (rare for Stu  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)...<P>Not really a location, but a fastener type.   At times, I wish the "tin nut" (my term) was never invented.  These are the cheap little "barely" hex nuts (of stamped sheet metal), used for lightly-loaded applications such as  attaching trim and heat shields.<P>These beauties deform when you try to wrench 'em off, turn cockeyed, crossthread, and even though they're trash at this point, <B>won't</B> let go of the stud they're on.  Being of very thin sheet metal, they also provide sharp edges to cut yourself on.<P>My favorite applications for "tin nuts" on the Fiero, are:  Heat shield attach, on the forward exhaust manifold (V6), the badge on the front fascia (twist arm into pretzel), and of course, the map pocket.    ||||reg||1
Z||000030||Will||03-29-2001||03:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B>On a serious note (rare for Stu   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)...<P>Not really a location, but a fastener type.   At times, I wish the "tin nut" (my term) was never invented.  These are the cheap little "barely" hex nuts (of stamped sheet metal), used for lightly-loaded applications such as  attaching trim and heat shields.<P>These beauties deform when you try to wrench 'em off, turn cockeyed, crossthread, and even though they're trash at this point, [b]won't</B> let go of the stud they're on.  Being of very thin sheet metal, they also provide sharp edges to cut yourself on.<P>My favorite applications for "tin nuts" on the Fiero, are:  Heat shield attach, on the forward exhaust manifold (V6), the badge on the front fascia (twist arm into pretzel), and of course, the map pocket.    [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The rack preload lock nut is like that. It's stamped out of 1/16" or so steel, and rounds when you put a wrench on it. I had to have a special wrench laser cut to get the darn thing off.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Seanh||03-29-2001||06:47 PM||||I think the upper exhaust manifold bolts on the front side are the most difficult. I droped a few tools in the engine compartment, but they all fall out the bottem for me. The few that don't seem to fall in the opening in the cradle. Try looking there for some of your tools. But, I think the hardest little thing to do is put the damn dipstick tube back in. I have search high and low, for 3 days after school and still can't locate the hole in the block. I even had someone start and rev the car to see if I could see oil comming out. Nope. Thats my luck. I broke the tube on our Lincoln, and oil gushed out it(about 1qt. a day) but when I want oil to fly out of the fiero, It won't do it!||||reg||1
Z||000032||Standard||03-30-2001||01:41 AM||||I'd have to agree with the lower control arm bolts... and that these cars love to eat tools.  I've lost at least two sockets working on my GT.  They just clunk down somewhere and disappear....||||reg||1
Z||000033||Jay||03-30-2001||05:48 PM||||Almost forgot one.  Anyone ever try to get their hand into the door panel to change the locks?  Damn! I must have about a hundred tiny cuts all over my hands.  That has got to be the worst!  Will this was a great post!!Most enjoyable!!||||reg||1
Z||000034||Bob Englert||03-30-2001||06:31 PM||||I haven't done much engine work on my GT's yet, so I have that to look forward to...<P>I would add a vote for the lower control arm bolts.  On my first 85 SE, I had to saw 2 of them out with a Sawzall, they were frozen to the inner sleeve of the bushing.<P>The sense of victory when you finally defeat the damn thing almost makes it worthwhile though.<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
